properties.statement:
A reflow2 server that holds many designs (a registry root):
- SHALL close a design that has had no request for its idle timeout (default about 15 minutes; configurable, and 0 means never);
- SHALL, when a new design is asked for at its open-design limit, close the least recently used design that has no request in flight, instead of refusing;
- SHALL refuse only when every open design has a request in flight. The refusal is 503 Service Unavailable with Retry-After and the same plain-language explanation, never 404, because 404 tells a client the design does not exist;
- SHALL NOT reopen a closed design in the same process until the previous copy has actually released its store, so a quick reopen can never collide with the copy being closed;
- SHALL end every session held on a design when it closes it. A client on an older MCP revision then gets 404 for that session and re-initializes, as the MCP spec requires; flo2's gateway already does.

WHY, in @ajs's words (2026-09-27), after the research on scaling: "Yes, let's build the now items in reflow2". What happened: on 2026-09-27 flo2.io's engine refused 3 of 11 designs as 404 because nothing it had opened was ever closed (fact:flo2-io-met-the-open-design-cap-with-no-idle-eviction-2026-09-27).

What current practice does (fact:how-current-practice-closes-and-moves-stateful-objects-2026-09-27): every system that serves many small stateful objects per process closes idle ones. Default timeouts: Akka 2 min, Orleans 15 min, Dapr 60 min. Past a limit it closes the least recently used one (Akka's active-entity-limit).
